WEBVTT

00:00.300 --> 00:04.270
Gas left in a tank all winter
doesn't stay the same gas.

00:05.140 --> 00:06.010
Here's what changes.

00:07.500 --> 00:13.650
Pump gas can be E ten: up to ten percent
ethanol, which is alcohol blended in.

00:14.060 --> 00:15.810
Ethanol attracts water.

00:17.020 --> 00:19.630
Enough water, and the fuel can separate.

00:20.300 --> 00:24.230
That's phase separation: it
stops being one even mix.

00:24.820 --> 00:26.910
Stabilizer can't put it back together.

00:28.140 --> 00:34.140
Even plain gas ages. Mercury says fuel
can start to oxidize in as little as two

00:34.160 --> 00:40.290
weeks. That means it starts to gum up
fuel lines, carburetors and injectors.

00:41.580 --> 00:44.530
Ethanol free gas skips the water problem.

00:45.100 --> 00:46.550
It still ages, though.

00:47.900 --> 00:49.330
Start with fresh fuel.

00:50.040 --> 00:54.560
If the boat will sit more than a couple
of weeks, add a marine stabilizer and run

00:54.580 --> 00:56.310
the engine to circulate it.

00:56.680 --> 00:59.370
Follow the manual for your serial number.

01:00.540 --> 01:01.930
Not sure about your tank?

01:02.700 --> 01:10.220
Fill out the service request at hbwservice.ca,
then drop off your boat by trailer or

01:10.240 --> 01:12.990
by water. We take it from there.

01:13.660 --> 01:16.790
We're closed December
first to April first.
